1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,560
Records made transparent data cheap to
declare. As soon as those data shapes form
a family — circle or rectangle, success or
failure, cash or card — a new risk
appears.

2
00:00:11,679 --> 00:00:24,719
Suppose we model shapes with an interface
and let anyone implement it. Drawing code
switches on runtime type, or uses
instanceof chains, and hopes it covered
every case. A new subtype arrives from
another package.

3
00:00:24,839 --> 00:00:35,079
The switch still compiles. The new shape
falls into a default that returns zero
area. The bug is quiet until a designer
adds a triangle and a dashboard goes
blank.

4
00:00:35,199 --> 00:00:49,799
So the question becomes: can a hierarchy
say, honestly, "these are the only
subtypes that exist"? Enums already closed
a set of constant instances. Here the
instances can be whole types — each
carrying different fields.

5
00:00:49,919 --> 00:01:02,079
That is the leap: from a flat vocabulary
to a closed family of shapes. Sealed
classes and interfaces answer that.
Sealing closes a hierarchy so switches and
readers can be exhaustive.

6
00:01:02,199 --> 00:01:10,119
The permits clause lists the allowed
subtypes. Look at this java for a moment.
Read it as a contract. Shape is sealed.

7
00:01:10,239 --> 00:01:16,519
Only Circle and Rect may implement it —
within the same-module and package rules
Java requires for permitted types.

8
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:27,359
Each permitted type is a record here,
which pairs cleanly with sealing: the
variants are data, the parent is the
closed sum of those variants. The payoff
shows up in switch.

9
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:37,520
Exhaustive switch checking becomes
possible because the compiler knows the
full set. Look at this java for a moment.
No default required when every permitted
subtype is handled.

10
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:50,680
Pattern matching binds c or r so you can
read components without a cast. Add
Triangle to permits later, and the
incomplete switch becomes a compile error
instead of a silent miss. What if the
domain must stay open?

11
00:01:50,859 --> 00:02:02,939
Then do not seal it. Open hierarchies stay
unsealed on purpose — plugin types, SPI
implementations, frameworks that expect
unknown subclasses. Sealing is for closed
worlds.

12
00:02:03,060 --> 00:02:05,700
Using it on an extension point fights the
product.

13
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:16,620
Sealed types and records together
encourage a style of domain modeling that
feels like algebraic data types: a small
set of known variants, each carrying its
fields, handled exhaustively.

14
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:28,180
A common misunderstanding is to seal a
type and then keep writing default
branches that swallow new cases. That
throws away the exhaustiveness gift.
Permit what the domain actually allows.

15
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:41,380
The same sealing idea shows up in results,
not only shapes. Look at this java for a
moment. Payment outcomes are a closed
domain. Callers should not invent a fourth
unofficial result by subclassing in
another jar.

16
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:54,340
Sealing says that out loud. Exhaustive
switch makes forgetting Pending a compile
error. Think of sealing as the sibling of
enums at a larger scale. Enums close a set
of constant instances.

17
00:02:54,460 --> 00:03:03,300
Sealed types close a set of subtypes that
may themselves carry data. Both exist so
the compiler can help you stay honest when
the domain is finite.

18
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:15,140
Once types and packages multiply across a
large codebase, another boundary question
appears above the package level: which
parts of the JDK and which parts of our
own system are allowed to see each other
at all?

19
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:21,520
Packages alone proved too weak for that.
That larger encapsulation story is modules
and JPMS.
